The Security Council,

Recalling all its previous resolutions concerning Haiti, in particular resolutions 2653 (2022), 2699 (2023), 2700 (2023), 2751 (2024), 2752 (2024), 2785 (2025), and 2793 (2025),

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity, and unity of Haiti,

Expressing grave concern about the extremely high levels of gang violence, criminal activities, and human rights abuses in Haiti, including kidnappings, homicides, sexual and gender-based violence, attacks on civilian infrastructure, and the continuous recruitment and use of children by armed gangs,

Stressing with deep concern the worsening humanitarian situation, food insecurity, and displacement of civilians, and underscoring that stability and security are essential prerequisites for long-term development and the holding of free and fair elections in Haiti,

Recognizing that the illicit flow of firearms, small arms, light weapons, and ammunition into Haiti constitutes a fundamental factor in fueling gang violence, destabilizing the country, and undermining the rule of law, and urging all Member States to take urgent, coordinated, and effective measures to stem illicit arms trafficking and diversion,

Welcoming the ongoing efforts of the Haitian National Police, the Haitian Armed Forces, the Multinational Security Support (MSS) mission, and the framework for transition to the Gang Suppression Force (GSF) authorized in resolution 2793 (2025), as well as the support provided by the United Nations Integrated Office in Haiti (BINUH) and regional organizations, including the Caribbean Community (CARICOM) and the Organization of American States (OAS),

Taking note of the reports and findings of the Panel of Experts established pursuant to resolution 2653 (2022), and emphasizing the importance of holding accountable those who finance, arm, and support criminal networks and gangs in Haiti,

Determining that the situation in Haiti continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security in the region,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Decides to renew the measures imposed by paragraphs 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, and 12 of resolution 2653 (2022), as reaffirmed and updated in resolution 2752 (2024), for an additional period of twelve months from the date of adoption of this resolution;

2. Decides to renew the measures concerning the arms embargo set forth in paragraph 14 of resolution 2699 (2023) and reinforced in paragraph 2 of resolution 2752 (2024) for an additional period of twelve months from the date of adoption of this resolution, and reaffirms that all Member States shall take the necessary measures to prevent the direct or indirect supply, sale, or transfer of arms and related materiel of all types, including small arms, light weapons, and ammunition, to non-governmental individuals and entities in Haiti, subject to the established exemptions for the United Nations, authorized international missions including BINUH and the GSF, and Haitian state security forces;

3. Calls upon all Member States, in particular regional Member States and transit countries, to strengthen customs, border management, and maritime and aerial surveillance to detect, intercept, and halt the illicit trafficking and diversion of arms, ammunition, and related materiel to and from Haiti, and encourages increased cooperation and sharing of information, including tracing requests with international law enforcement entities such as INTERPOL;

4. Directs the Committee established pursuant to resolution 2653 (2022) to update and expand, as appropriate, the sanctions list by considering designations of individuals and entities that engage in or support acts that threaten the peace, security, or stability of Haiti, including those who provide financial, material, or logistical support, or supply illicit weapons and ammunition, to armed gangs and criminal networks;

5. Decides to extend the mandate of the Panel of Experts appointed pursuant to paragraph 21 of resolution 2653 (2022) for a period of thirteen months from the date of adoption of this resolution, and decides that the mandate of the Panel of Experts shall continue to encompass the tasks set forth in paragraph 21 of resolution 2653 (2022) and modified by subsequent resolutions;

6. Requests the Panel of Experts to provide to the Council, after discussion with the Committee, an interim report by 15 April 2026, a final report no later than 15 August 2026, and periodic updates to the Committee, particularly on the sources, routes, and financial mechanisms supporting illicit arms transfers to gangs;

7. Urges all parties, Member States, and international and regional partners, as well as the Haitian authorities, to cooperate fully with the Panel of Experts and ensure the safety of its members and unhindered access to persons, documents, and sites relevant to the execution of its mandate;

8. Requests the Secretary-General to conduct, in close consultation with the Committee and the Haitian Government, an assessment of progress achieved regarding the key benchmarks established in resolution 2653 (2022), no later than 15 August 2026, with a view to adjusting, suspending, or lifting the sanctions measures as appropriate;

9. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

